{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# ML-02 — Research Question and Provisional Lane\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Di-pesh/flyinterm/blob/main/work/notebooks/w01_research_question.ipynb)\n\n## 1. My lane (or freestyle) and why\n\nI am taking the refresh-triage lane: prioritizing which content pages are most likely to decline, so a content team can decide where to refresh first. This is a strong fit for the FlyRank data because the repo contains a 30k-page sample with repeated client-level behavior, real traffic metrics, and a clear label derived from recent trend direction. The decision is operationally valuable: a wrong call means spending time on a page that did not need attention, while a good call helps the team protect organic discoverability before performance falls further.\n\nThe data is suitable for a supervised learning lane because each row represents a page with a static content profile plus recent search/analytics behavior, and the target is defined from recent impression trend direction.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "root = Path.cwd().resolve()\n",
    "candidates = [root, *root.parents]\n",
    "raw_path = None\n",
    "for p in candidates:\n",
    "    candidate = p / 'data' / 'raw' / 'content_refresh_anonymized.csv'\n",
    "    if candidate.exists():\n",
    "        raw_path = candidate\n",
    "        break\n",
    "if raw_path is None:\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError('Expected data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv in the repo root.')\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path)\n",
    "print(f'Rows: {len(df):,}')\n",
    "print(f'Clients: {df[\'client_id\'].nunique():,}')\n",
    "print(f'Columns: {len(df.columns)}')\n",
    "print(df[['content_id', 'client_id', 'impressions_90d', 'sessions_90d', 'trend_direction']].head().to_string(index=False))\n",
    "\n",
    "declining_rate = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').mean()\n",
    "print(f'Base decline rate: {declining_rate:.3f}')\n",
    "print(f'Average 90d impressions: {df[\'impressions_90d\'].mean():,.0f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. The question: decision, action, cost of a wrong call\n\nThe decision is: which content pages deserve refresh effort before they continue to lose search visibility? The team that acts on this is the content operations team, or the growth/content production team reviewing the queue. A wrong recommendation is not catastrophic, but it is expensive in wasted editorial effort and delayed prioritization. If the model over-selects stale or low-value pages, the team spends time on low-impact items instead of pages with real falloff risk. If it under-selects, opportunity is missed and the decline cascade continues.\n\nThis makes the problem a ranking and triage problem rather than a pure prediction problem: maximize the chance of catching content likely to decline while keeping the queue usable for manual review.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path)\n",
    "\n",
    "decline_flag = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down')\n",
    "print('Pages flagged as declining:', int(decline_flag.sum()))\n",
    "print('Share flagged as declining:', decline_flag.mean())\n",
    "print('Top traffic pages:')\n",
    "print(df.nlargest(5, 'impressions_90d')[['content_id', 'client_id', 'impressions_90d', 'trend_direction']].to_string(index=False))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Quick look at the data (2-3 real numbers)\n\nThis lane looks promising because the sample is large enough to learn signal from repeated client patterns, and the label has enough variation to make ranking useful. The numbers below show the rough scale: the dataset includes multiple clients, a large number of content items, and a non-trivial baseline rate for pages in decline.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path)\n",
    "rr = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower()\n",
    "stats = {\n",
    "    'rows': len(df),\n",
    "    'clients': df['client_id'].nunique(),\n",
    "    'decline_rate': rr.eq('down').mean(),\n",
    "    'median_impressions_90d': df['impressions_90d'].median(),\n",
    "    'median_sessions_90d': df['sessions_90d'].median(),\n",
    "}\n",
    "for k, v in stats.items():\n",
    "    print(f'{k}: {v}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Careful words: what I can and can't claim\n\nI can claim that the model is useful as a decision-support signal for prioritizing refresh work and identifying content pages that deserve a human review. I can measure whether the chosen features and model rank likely decliners better than a naive baseline, and I can report how consistent that ranking is across client groups.\n\nI cannot claim causal proof that traffic decline is caused by any single content attribute or that a recommended refresh is guaranteed to improve rankings. This work is directional and observational. It is useful for action planning, not for proving causality or for making a high-stakes automated content decision without a human in the loop.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# This section records the main claim safely and ties it back to the observed data.\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(raw_path)\n",
    "decline_rate = df['trend_direction'].astype(str).str.lower().eq('down').mean()\n",
    "print('Observed direction: this project can support ranking and triage, not causal proof.')\n",
    "print(f'Observed decline base rate in the sample: {decline_rate:.3f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
